  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthen monitoring assessments of internal displacement in HaitiStrengthen monitoring assessments of internal displacement in Haiti</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gang violence in Haiti claimed 5,600 lives in 2024 and displaced over a million people, half of them children. According to IOM&apos;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM), 83% of internally displaced persons (IDPs) are hosted by families, while 17% reside in precarious sites like schools and churches. Displacement has surged, particularly in the capital and Artibonite, tripling since June 2023. To address this crisis, the Haitian government, through the Economic and Social Assistance Fund (FAES), requests the completion of DTM Round 12 to provide up-to-date data for effective social protection and humanitarian response. This data guides interventions, improve coordination, and optimize resource allocation. Round 12 will produce a detailed analysis report, a summary fact sheet, raw datasets, a list of spontaneous sites, and an interactive multi-layered map to enhance response planning and support evidence-based stabilization efforts.</narrative>
  </description>
